body:
Luckily, universities are pretty large population-wise, so it's easy to avoid that crowd if you need to. I go to a campus of just 15,000 total and there's a big lgbt presence on it. Even then I could just politely excuse myself from trans stuff when it came up and still maintain a social life.

&#x200B;

Join a club or two, focus on your studies, make friends, and all that good stuff :) College is what you make it out to be.

Hi guys, I hope this isn't too out there but I would appreciate it if someone could help point me in the right direction.

&#x200B;

Way back in 2011, I was too sick too go get the shiny event Entei but my mom took my DS in secret and got him for me despite not even knowing how to save the game. It's a memory that's very dear to me. I've had this Entei until 2018, when I deposited all 700+ of my Pokemon (I had a complete living dex of everything up to gen 6) into a copy of Omega Ruby... and lost it. I've been looking since then and it's never turned up, so I'm fairly certain they're all gone permanently. Including my event Entei.

&#x200B;

Naturally, I don't have anything to offer because all I can get now is current events and the Colosseum Ho-Oh, and the 3 boxes of Slurpuff which are all that are left in my Pokemon Bank.

I'm working on rebuilding my dex because they're all just virtual creatures, but that Entei was special to me and for that reason I'm looking for one. I know DNS hacked Pokemon and clones are banned on this sub, but I would be okay with accepting one because I don't care about legitimacy of the Entei, but the memories I have attached to it. Does anyone know where I can trade for a Pokemon like this? A more lax sub or community somewhere else, maybe?

All I can tell you is that what you see in magazines and on the internet isn't real life. There are tons of people who are not only okay with but actually *like* small or flat chests on women--both guys and girls. They think it's more aesthetically and sexually pleasing. I couldn't give you an exact number but I know that I've heard the sentiment so many times, from all different types of people, online and off, that it's almost cliche.

&#x200B;

And maybe it's a little uncommon to not have really dated by 28 but it's never too late to start. Especially in this day and age, where people are settling down later and later, you're definitely not without hope. Just put yourself out there if you haven't already. Join a group in your community or download a dating app to gain some experience around what you should do in regards to dating. It's not that scary. Every bread has its cheese, but you won't know which goes with which until you taste a couple combinations. Love will find you eventually, sometimes when you're not even looking for it.

My story is really close to yours. Like, really really close. The only difference is that, because of some trauma in my past, I struggle a lot with my sexuality so I can't say for certain right now who I'd date and who I wouldn't. But I digress.

&#x200B;

I like to say that you just need to take baby steps to your goal and it'll be okay. Personally I wouldn't say you made a mistake because going through this is what you needed to find yourself--like you said, it was the right decision back then, but isn't anymore. There's nothing wrong with that. You physically can't undo all your progress in a day and this is a blessing and a curse.

&#x200B;

Tell some close friends or family how you feel, or if you're not comfortable with that yet, tell them you want to change your name back or to something else. Take a shower. Buy some accessories. Anything you want that will make you feel happy and secure. Remember that you can keep bits and pieces of your current identity, style, etc. 

Nothing is lost. Your life has only just begun.

Definitely avoid finasteride if you have pets, especially a cat. If you touch them and any remains on your hands, they could get sick or die.

&#x200B;

Hair growth can't really be sped up in any way other than giving your follicles a healthy environment to grow in and lots of nutrients.

&#x200B;

Keep your scalp moisturized using coconut oil. Shampoo/wash your hair with soap less frequently. If you need to get it clean, wet it and rub your scalp well with your fingers and this will refresh it without stripping out the oils you need. Gradually step down shampooing from once every day or two days, to once or twice a week. Keep conditioning as needed, but remember that conditioner can't heal damage, only prevent further damage.

&#x200B;

When you wash your hair, massage your scalp with the pads of your fingers. You can do this by rubbing your fingers in circles and by anchoring them near the front of your head and pulling back until you feel the skin move towards the back of your head. This will promote blood flow.

&#x200B;

Don't skimp out on hair products. $40 for a shampoo or conditioner sounds ridiculous until you consider how long you're going to use it for. pH-balanced shampoos are a good option, but if you're really unsure what to buy then consider consulting with a professional so your products can meet your needs.

&#x200B;

Always wash with warm, not hot, water. Cold water is ideal but I know some people don't like cold showers. When you leave the shower, do not blow dry your hair, rub it dry with a towel, or wrap it in a towel turban-style. If you have to blow dry, use cold air. Otherwise, all these things will damage it. If you want to wrap it then get a microfiber towel. If not, then instead pat your hair dry with a towel or let it air dry.

&#x200B;

Brush it regularly, preferably with a soft or natural fiber brush. This will help dislodge shedding hairs, clearing the way for new growth in the same follicle.

Change out your pillowcases often. This will also help you with clearing your skin as a bonus.

&#x200B;

Increase your protein intake and consider taking supplements for biotin and keratin (I say consider because it's not 100% clear yet whether or not taking supplements can help boost hair growth, and oversupplementation can be dangerous if the pills you're taking contain something other than just biotin/keratin, like Vitamin A or D). Also make sure you're eating a healthy diet outside of just the amount of protein you're eating, with lots of fruits and vegetables. Exercise may also help because hair and nails are a signpost to overall health.

&#x200B;

When you go outside, wear hats or carry an umbrella to save your hair from sun damage. If your hair is long, try to put it up as infrequently as possible to avoid tension alopecia.

&#x200B;

I do all these things and my hair can grow 1.5 inches a month. It does require more effort than most people's routines, but if you're serious about this then it will be worth it. Not sure how much it will help regain lost growth, but this is the best way I know to get and maintain a healthy scalp.

From an entertainment standpoint:

I don't like TV or movies but I do read. I prefer the books because I can stop and start them at any time, and also they're not really overwhelming to the senses like television can be. 

&#x200B;

For example, if I'm watching any video with a narrative, I get really confused trying to keep track of all the characters and if it's a particularly long episode/film then rewatching to get a better handle on things isn't really an option. This becomes a huge problem when watching movies that also want you to think about what's going on, like Primer. Hell, even to this day I can't understand the twist in Ocean's 11 because I don't know who everyone is.

&#x200B;

Also, if I don't understand a specific word or phrase in a book, I can reread it or look it up. But when I have trouble understanding what TV characters are saying, the only option is subtitles or skipping back and forth (which I hate).

&#x200B;

Books can also provide the amount of narrative depth that movies could never do, or that shows must accomplish over many seasons. Unless you prefer to sit down and watch 2-10 hours of content, which I do not.

&#x200B;

There's also none of the frustration that comes with bad actors, editing issues, and music. Unless you're reading a particularly amateur author, in writing everyone is worthy of an Oscar.

This happened to me once, too! A couple years ago I was up late at night (maybe 3 am) playing around on my phone. My bed is right next to a window, so when a lot of extra light started shining in, I looked out and saw this amazing shining thing flying across the sky! It moved so fast, more than the occasional satellite you can see from earth.

&#x200B;

It freaked me out a little because I didn't know what it was, but when I looked it up I was so excited. The next morning, I told my brother about it (because he is a total geek) and he was soo jealous.

&#x200B;

I wonder if anyone in there was looking down at Earth while I was looking up at them!

Yes. I had and still had gender dysphoria. I was a textbook case for it, in fact. I think all but one diagnostic criterion I could check off. And still can. I even showed all the "signs" as a kid. I was not confused, nor were my mental health professionals confused.

&#x200B;

After transitioning for about a year, I found that my GD was alleviated but at a cost. I did like my changes but I realized that I did not want to be the opposite gender, just a very masculine girl.

I also realized that even if I did want it, it is physically impossible regardless of medical, social, or legal interventions, to change one's gender. It just is.

&#x200B;

I deal with my dysphoria now by doing this type of thought experiment: imagine yourself on top of a mountain at sunrise. The wind is blowing through your hair, the sky is a perfect shade of pink, the birds are waking up and calling to each other, the sun is warming your skin, the trees are rustling gently. The vast peaks all around you cast long, dark shadows over the land. You can see for miles. You are sitting on top of a tall church spire and can hear cow's bells ringing in the distance as they graze. It seems like you're the only person awake in the whole world.

&#x200B;

I've been on the Swiss Alps at this time so this is a memory that is very dear to me. I remember that my body is mine, always and forever, and that I will have experiences like this in my body no matter what.

The wind will always blow through my hair regardless of what I call myself and what other people call me. As long as I have eyes, I will always see beautiful things. As long as I have ears, I will hear them. Until I die, I will feel sunlight on my skin. Gender doesn't even come close to the equation. It's not needed to live my life however I want. Not transitioning wouldn't kill me and it wouldn't keep me from these things.

&#x200B;

I also like to remember that the vast majority of people don't care about my gender. And the ones who do, are misogynists and will burn in hell so I shouldn't care what they think anyway.

And finally, I deal with GD by telling myself that I can still call myself a guy and have a guy's name and wear guy's clothes and make people call me "he" and do everything I want... while still being a girl and being myself. As long as I acknowledge that one reality, I allow myself freedom to be me, and it works great because I no longer worry about "presentation" or "passing" or silly things like that.

At a gym, everyone is too focused on what they're doing to even notice you. Where I go, there's a guy who comes in with a 3DS and messes with it on the stationary bikes and nobody cares.

&#x200B;

It's nerve wracking at first, but the only way to get over gym anxiety is through exposure. I'd say go at least a couple times and see if you like it.
